#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119532 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a4d10f94-98d4-53d8-a35c-e65a06dd2c55" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the LU Factorization Using a Single Elementary Row Operation"}] :question/id #uuid "f7324b66-d8f7-5bdb-9786-4160808f2a7b" :question/math-academy-id "e-4768" :question/problem "Given that\n$\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 6 & -2\\end{bmatrix}$\nhas the LU decomposition $A=LU$, then $L=$" :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U$, we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= \\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 6 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}R_{3} &:=R_{3} + (-2)R_{2} & ⟺\\end{vmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}^(⏞)^(E_{1}) \\cdot A \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= U\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U$. Now, since $E_1A=U$, we can invert $E_1$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE_{1}A &= U \\\\\nA &= E_{1}^{-1}U \\\\\nA &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}^{-1}U \\\\\nA &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}_(⏟)_(L)\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix}_(⏟)_(U)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119533 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c91fb2b-5607-555d-b897-f0e76765dfa6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Lower Triangular Matrix Given the Elementary Row Operations"}] :question/id #uuid "58955d86-8a94-5174-83fa-072314037298" :question/math-academy-id "e-4769" :question/problem "Suppose that a $3 \\times 3$ matrix $F$ is reduced to row echelon form using the following elementary row operations, applied in the order in which they appear:\n$R_2:=R_2+R_1, \\qquad R_3:=R_3+2R_1$\nGiven that $F$ has the LU factorization $F=LU$, then $L=$" :question/worked-solution "Applying\n\n$$\nR_2:=R_2 + {\\color{red}1} \\cdot R_1\n$$\n\nand then\n\n$$\nR_3:=R_3+ {\\color{red}2} \\cdot R_1\n$$\n\nis equivalent to multiplying $F$ from the left by the following elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, since $E_2E_1F=U$, we can invert $E_2$ and $E_1$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE_{2}E_{1}F &= U \\\\\nE_{1}F &= E_{2}^{-1}U \\\\\nF &= E_{1}^{-1}E_{2}^{-1}U \\\\\nF &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}^{-1}U \\\\\nF &= (\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix})U \\\\\nF &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}_(⏟)_(L) \\cdot U\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119534 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e460dcd-59cf-54d2-80e6-c0d72b6bafdd" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the LU Factorization Using Two or More Elementary Row Operations"}] :question/id #uuid "50685ac4-9980-5841-a669-783f6086a9dd" :question/math-academy-id "e-4770" :question/problem "Given that\n$\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 8 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 11 & -6 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 16\\end{bmatrix}$\nhas the LU decomposition $A=LU$, then $L=$" :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U$, we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= \\begin{bmatrix}4 & 8 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 11 & -6 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 16\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}R_{2} &:=R_{2} + (-\\frac{5}{4})R_{1} & ⟺ & \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{5}{4} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}^(⏞)^(E_{1}) \\cdot A \\\\ ∼\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -6 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 16\\end{bmatrix} & R_{3} &:=R_{3} + 4R_{2} & ⟺\\end{vmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}^(⏞)^(E_{2}) \\cdot E_{1} \\cdot A \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= U\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U$. Now, since $E_2E_1A=U$, we can invert $E_2$ and $E_1$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE_{2}E_{1}A &= U \\\\\nE_{1}A &= E_{2}^{-1}U \\\\\nA &= E_{1}^{-1}E_{2}^{-1}U \\\\\nA &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{5}{4} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}^{-1}U \\\\\nA &= (\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{5}{4} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 1\\end{bmatrix})U \\\\\nA &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{5}{4} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}_(⏟)_(L)\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -8\\end{bmatrix}_(⏟)_(U)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120114 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e460dcd-59cf-54d2-80e6-c0d72b6bafdd" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the LU Factorization Using Two or More Elementary Row Operations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a63866ed-9b40-5485-9cd8-7a78f3808192" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117205} {:answer/id #uuid "af02c1c5-96de-5a22-b251-3af3077eeb4a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 1759218611